","The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by:

\[
\lambda_0 / (2n_f)
\]

where:
- \(\lambda_0\) is the wavelength of the incident light,
- \(n_f\) is the refractive of the front (outside) medium.

For a waveguide, the decay length is also given by:

\[
\lambda_0 / (2(n_{\text{guide}} - n_f))
\]

where \(n_{\text{gap}}\) is the refractive index of the waveguide cladding.

For a waveguide, the decay length is:

\[
\lambda_0 / (2(n_{\text{core}} - n_{\text{clad}})
\]

Given:
- \(\lambda_0 = 1 \, \mu m = 1000 \, \text{nm}\)
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective index of the waveguide, and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the surrounding film.

The decay length is:

\[
 \text{decay length} = \frac{\lambda_0}{2(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f)}} = \frac{1000 \, \text{nm}}{2 \times 0.3} = 1666 \, \text{nm}
\]

