","To solve this problem, we need to consider the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a constant speed relative to another observer. The time experienced by the object (the spaceship) will appear slower compared to the time experienced by the observer (Earth).

First, let's find the time experienced by the observer on Earth. The time dilation formula is:

Δt' = Δt / √(1 - v^2/c^2)

where Δt' is the time experienced by the observer on Earth, Δt is the time experienced by the spaceship, v is the relative speed of the spaceship, and c is the speed of light.

We are given that the time on the passenger in the cockpit is 3 years when the ship returns to Earth. This is the time experienced by the spaceship (Δt = 3 years). We are also given that the spaceship's speed is 0.6c (v = 0.6c). Now we can calculate the time experienced by the observer on Earth (Δt'):

Δt' = 3 years / √(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
Δt' = 3 years / √(1 - 0.36)
Δt' = 3 years / √(0.64)
Δt' = 3 years / 0.8
Δt' = 3.75 years

So, the time experienced on Earth is 3.75 years.

