","To compute the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system, we use the formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
u(x, y, z) & v(x, y, z) & w(x, z)
\end{vmatrix},
\]
where \(\mathbf{V} = \mathbf{i} u(x, y, z) + \mathbf{j} v(x, y, z) + \mathbf{k} w(x, y, z)\). 

Since \(\mathbf{V}\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), we have:
\[
u(x, y, z) = u(x, y), \quad v(x, y, z) = v(x, y), \quad w(x, y, z) = 0.
\]
Substituting these into the curl formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \0 \\
u(x, y) & v(x, y) & 0
\end{vvmatrix}.
\]
Expanding the determinant along the third component:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot 0\right) \hat{i} - \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot v(x, y)\right) \hat{j} + \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot v(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot u(x, y)\right) \hat{k}.
\]
Simplifying:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = 0 \hat{i} - 0 \hat{j} + \left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right) \hat{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right) \hat{k}.
\]

The components of the curl are:
\[
\mathbf{r} \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y} \end{vmatrix}.
\]

Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is \(\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right) \hat{k}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
