","The lifetime of a particle is related to its width in the energy spectrum, as given by the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle:  
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t = \hbar c
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta E\) is the energy uncertainty (width of the decay mode),  
- \(\Delta t\) is the lifetime of the particle,  
- \hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.0 \times 10^{-34}\) J·s),  
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3 \times 10^8\) m/s).  

Substituting the given lifetime (\(\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s) and solving for \(\Delta E\):  
\[
\Delta E = \frac{\hbar c}{\Delta t} = \frac{(1.0 \times 10^{-34} \times 3 \times 10^8)}{2.0 \times 10^{-23}} = 1.5 \times 10^{-10} \text{ eV}
\]  
Convert to MeV:  
\[
\Delta E = 1.5 \times 10^{-10} \text{ eV} \times \frac{1000 \text{ eV}}{1 \text{ MeV}} = 1.5 \times 10^{-7} \text{ MeV}
\]  
Thus, the width of the decay mode is \(\boxed{1.5 \times 10^{-7} \text{ MeV}}\).  

Thus, the total range of energies of the decay mode is:  
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} - 1.5 \times 10^{-7} \text{ MeV} \approx 1385 \text{ MeV}
\]  
The range is very small, so the actual measurement would be very close to the given rest energy.  

